EXPERIMENT - 1.1 -> Contains Duplicate (217):
AIM: 
To determine whether an integer array contains any duplicate elements.

ALGORITHM:
1. Start.
2. Input the size of the array n.
3. Input the n elements of the array.
4. Create an empty HashSet (or HashMap).
5. Traverse the array from the first element to the last.
6. For each element:
  ->Check if the element is already present in the HashSet.
  ->If it is present, return true because a duplicate is found.
  ->Otherwise, insert the element into the HashSet.
7. If the entire array is traversed without finding any duplicate, return false.
8. Stop.

CODE:
class Solution {
public:
    bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        for(int i = 1; i< nums.size(); i++){
            if(nums[i] == nums[i-1]){
                return true;
            }    
        }
        return false;
    }
};


EXPERIMENT - 1.2 -> Contains Duplicate (219):
AIM: 
To determine whether an array contains duplicate elements whose indices differ by at most k.

ALGORITHM:
1. Start.
2. Input the size of the array n.
3. Input the n elements of the array.
4. Input the value of k.
5. Create an empty HashMap to store each element and its most recent index.
6. Traverse the array from index 0 to n-1.
7. For each element:
  -> Check if the element already exists in the HashMap.
  -> If it exists:
       -> Find the difference between the current index and the stored index.
       -> If the difference is less than or equal to k, return true.
  -> Update the HashMap by storing the current index of the element.
8. If the traversal completes without finding such a pair, return false.
9. Stop

CODE:
class Solution {
public:
    bool containsNearbyDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        unordered_map<int, int> mp;

        for(int i = 0; i<nums.size(); i++){
            if(mp.find(nums[i]) != mp.end()){
                if(i - mp[nums[i]] <= k)
                return true;
            }
            mp[nums[i]] = i;
        }
        return false;
    }
};
